<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="guides/finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">I Learned This the Expensive Way</h2>
  <p>
    Second year freelancing. Big project, new client. Sent the invoice with Net 30 because that's what everyone does,
    right? Day 30 comes. Nothing. Day 45. Nothing. Day 60, I finally call. "Oh, we're on a Net 60 cycle here."
  </p>
  <p>
    That $4,800 invoice? Didn't hit my account until day 73. Meanwhile, rent was due in Chiang Mai, my credit card was
    maxed, and I'm doing math on whether I can stretch a bag of rice for two weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now I know better. Net 30 isn't some universal rule. It's what slow-paying clients want you to think is normal.
    Your payment terms are negotiable. And picking the wrong ones? Creates <a
      href="/tools/best-accounting-software-freelancers">cash flow problems</a> you don't need to have.</p>

  <!-- Common Payment Terms -->
  <h2 id="payment-terms">Common Payment Terms Explained</h2>

  <h3>Due on Receipt</h3>
  <p>
    Payment expected immediately upon receiving the invoice. Reality is most clients pay within seven to fourteen days.
    Best for new clients and small projects and retail or consumer services.
  </p>

  <h3>Net Seven or Net Ten</h3>
  <p>
    Payment due within seven or ten days. Best for ongoing relationships and smaller invoices and service businesses.
  </p>

  <h3>Net Fifteen</h3>
  <p>
    Payment due within fifteen days. Best for a balanced approach. Reasonable for clients and good for cash flow. Often
    a good middle ground for freelancers.
  </p>

  <h3>Net Thirty</h3>
  <p>
    Payment due within thirty days. Best for established relationships and larger companies and B2B standard. Warning
    that industry default doesn't mean it's right for you.
  </p>

  <h3>Net Forty-Five or Net Sixty or Net Ninety</h3>
  <p>
    Extended payment windows of forty-five or sixty or ninety days. Best for large enterprises with accounting cycles.
    Rarely appropriate for freelancers unless compensated.
  </p>

  <!-- Net 30 vs Net 15 -->
  <h2 id="net-30-vs-15">Net Thirty vs Net Fifteen Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Choosing between Net thirty and Net fifteen significantly impacts your cash flow.
  </p>

  <h3>Net Fifteen Advantages</h3>
  <p>
    Faster cash flow since money arrives two weeks sooner. Less financing needed reducing need for credit or savings
    buffer. Better collection rates since shorter terms often mean better payment behavior. Signals professionalism and
    shows you run a serious business.
  </p>

  <h3>Net Thirty Advantages</h3>
  <p>
    Client-friendly with more time to process invoices. Industry expectation that's standard in many B2B relationships.
    Larger projects where it's reasonable for invoices over five thousand dollars. Enterprise clients where some large
    companies require it.
  </p>

  <h3>Real Impact on Cash Flow</h3>
  <p>
    With ten thousand dollars in monthly billings. Net fifteen means you typically have around five thousand to
    seventy-five hundred dollars of receivables outstanding. Net thirty means you typically have around ten thousand to
    fifteen thousand outstanding. Net sixty means you might have around twenty thousand plus waiting to come in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The difference compounds when you have multiple clients on different cycles.
  </p>

  <!-- Due on Receipt -->
  <h2 id="due-on-receipt">When to Use Due on Receipt</h2>
  <p>
    Due on Receipt isn't aggressive. It's appropriate for many situations.
  </p>

  <h3>Good Candidates for DOR</h3>
  <p>
    New clients until they prove reliable payment behavior. One-time projects with no ongoing relationship to maintain.
    Small invoices under five hundred to one thousand dollars. Consumer clients meaning individuals versus businesses.
    Rush work when you prioritized their project.
  </p>

  <h3>How to Frame It</h3>
  <p>
    Don't apologize for DOR terms. Professional language includes payment is due upon receipt of this invoice. Please
    remit payment within seven days. We accept payment methods and payment is due immediately.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most clients won't push back. And those who do may be warning signs.
  </p>

  <!-- Early Payment Discounts -->
  <h2 id="early-payment">Early Payment Discounts</h2>
  <p>
    Early payment discounts incentivize faster payment with a small price reduction.
  </p>

  <h3>Common Discount Structures</h3>
  <p>
    Two ten Net thirty means two percent discount if paid within ten days otherwise full amount due in thirty. One ten
    Net thirty means one percent discount for payment within ten days. Three fifteen Net forty-five means three percent
    discount if paid within fifteen days with full amount in forty-five.
  </p>

  <h3>The Math Behind Discounts</h3>
  <p>
    A two ten Net thirty discount means clients save two percent by paying twenty days early. Annualized that's roughly
    two percent divided by twenty days times three sixty-five days equals about thirty-six point five percent annual
    return.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clients with strong cash positions should always take this discount. It's better than any investment return. For you
    it's paying thirty-six percent interest to get cash twenty days early.
  </p>

  <h3>When Discounts Make Sense</h3>
  <p>
    You need cash urgently but consider if this is a recurring problem. You're working with slow-paying clients who
    respond to incentives. Your margins are high enough to absorb the discount.
  </p>

  <h3>When to Skip Discounts</h3>
  <p>
    Your cash flow is healthy. Clients already pay on time. Margins are thin.
  </p>

  <!-- Late Payment Fees -->
  <h2 id="late-fees">Late Payment Fees</h2>
  <p>
    Late fees discourage delayed payment and compensate you for the inconvenience.
  </p>

  <h3>Standard Late Fee Structures</h3>
  <p>
    Percentage-based at one to one point five percent per month or twelve to eighteen percent annually. Flat fee of
    twenty-five to fifty dollars per late invoice. Combination of flat fee plus percentage for continued lateness.
  </p>

  <h3>How to Implement</h3>
  <p>
    State terms clearly on invoices and contracts and statements of work. Get agreement since client should acknowledge
    terms before work begins. Apply consistently and don't waive for some and not others. Communicate first and send
    reminder before applying fee.
  </p>

  <h3>Sample Language</h3>
  <p>
    Payments not received within X days of invoice date are subject to a late fee of one point five percent per month or
    eighteen percent annually. We reserve the right to suspend work on ongoing projects until outstanding balances are
    cleared.
  </p>

  <h3>The Psychology of Late Fees</h3>
  <p>
    The goal isn't to collect late fees. It's to encourage on-time payment. Many freelancers include late fee language
    but rarely enforce it. The mere presence of the policy improves payment behavior.
  </p>

  <!-- Choosing Terms by Client -->
  <h2 id="client-terms">Choosing Terms by Client Type</h2>

  <h3>Small Businesses and Startups</h3>
  <p>
    Recommended Net fifteen or fifty percent deposit plus Net fifteen on completion. Why is cash flow is often tight and
    shorter terms help both parties. Risk of higher risk of payment issues so use deposits.
  </p>

  <h3>Mid-Size Companies</h3>
  <p>
    Recommended Net fifteen to Net thirty. Why is they usually have accounts payable processes but not bureaucratic. Tip
    is build relationship with AP contact for smoother payments.
  </p>

  <h3>Enterprise Clients</h3>
  <p>
    Recommended Net thirty but may require Net forty-five to sixty. Why is large companies have fixed payment cycles and
    processes. Tip is price in the longer payment cycle and request deposit if possible.
  </p>

  <h3>Individual Consumers</h3>
  <p>
    Recommended Due on Receipt or prepayment. Why is no accounts payable department and payment is personal decision.
    Tip is accept <a href="/tools/wise-vs-paypal-vs-payoneer">credit cards</a> for immediate payment.
  </p>

  <!-- Communication Tips -->
  <h2 id="communication">How to Communicate Terms</h2>

  <h3>In Your Contract</h3>
  <p>
    Payment terms should appear in every contract. Deposit requirements covering amount and when due. Payment schedule
    for milestones. Final payment terms. Late fee policy. Accepted payment methods.
  </p>

  <h3>On Your Invoice</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/best-invoicing-tools">Clear invoice language</a>. Payment Terms: Net fifteen. Due Date: Specific
    Date. Please pay by Date. Late fee policy
    reminder. Payment instructions and methods.
  </p>

  <h3>During Sales Conversations</h3>
  <p>
    Mention terms before they sign. We require a fifty percent deposit to begin and the balance due upon completion. Our
    standard terms are Net fifteen from invoice date. Do these payment terms work for your process?
  </p>
  <p>
    Bringing it up naturally prevents surprises and shows professionalism.
  </p>

  <!-- Handling Late Payments -->
  <h2 id="late-payments">What to Do When Clients Pay Late</h2>

  <h3>Gentle Reminder on Day One to Seven Past Due</h3>
  <p>
    Hi Name I wanted to follow up on invoice number XXX dated date. Our records show it's past due. Could you let me
    know when to expect payment? Happy to resend the invoice if needed.
  </p>

  <h3>Firmer Follow-Up on Day Eight to Fourteen</h3>
  <p>
    Hi Name following up on invoice number XXX which is now X days past due. Please remit payment at your earliest
    convenience. Per our agreement late fees of X percent will apply to balances over X days.
  </p>

  <h3>Final Notice on Day Fifteen to Thirty</h3>
  <p>
    Dear Name invoice number XXX for dollar amount is now X days overdue. Payment is required within seven days to avoid
    late fees or work suspension or collection action. Please contact me immediately if there's an issue.
  </p>

  <h3>When to Escalate</h3>
  <p>
    Thirty plus days means phone call to discuss directly. Sixty plus days means formal demand letter. Ninety plus days
    means consider collections or legal action.
  </p>

  <h3>Prevention Is Better</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide">Vet clients</a> before accepting work. Require deposits on new
    relationships. Set up automatic payment reminders.
    Invoice promptly since delays on your end delay their payment.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Take Control of Your Cash Flow</h2>
  <p>
    Payment terms aren't just administrative details. They're business decisions that affect your <a
      href="/tools/best-expense-tracking-apps">financial health</a>. The
    right terms protect your cash flow while maintaining good client relationships.
  </p>
  <p>
    Key takeaways. Net thirty isn't mandatory so choose terms that work for your business. Shorter terms like Net
    fifteen or DOR often make more sense for freelancers. Always require deposits from new clients. State terms clearly
    in contracts and invoices. Have a system for following up on late payments.
  </p>
  <p>
    You did the work. You deserve to get paid on time.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
